{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793563873,"nonce":"R38zaA48TDCauXepjJoniZaVU54=","message":"Narrowed on the Mac (arm64, zypy nbody_modified, ZYPY_LLVM=1), nothing fixed:\n\n- Not CPU contention: a spinning neighbour process leaves the Cranelift-only run at 5.0 ms; the promotion thread at QOS_CLASS_UTILITY changes nothing; at QOS_CLASS_BACKGROUND the 7.4 ms phase lasts as long as the (now slow) install does, 66 ms and more, and ends when the install lands. The slowdown follows the install being in progress, whoever runs it and at what priority.\n- Not the helper installs: skipping zb_list_get_unchecked_f64 and zb_list_expect_len_f64 (the first two LLVM installs) leaves the 7.5 ms phase as it was.\n- `sample` of the program thread in the persistent phase (background promoter) and in the settled phases: everything runs inside one call_native_through into JIT code, with libm `pow` at 39% of the samples in the slow phase against 35% (Cranelift, 5.0 ms/iter) and 38% (settled LLVM, 4.0 ms/iter): pow costs 2.9 ms per iteration in the slow phase and 1.5 to 1.75 in the others. The kernel raises x to a fractional power. So the program's time in pow nearly doubles while an install is in progress; something that differs per thread or per process while LLVM compiles (floating-point state, or the libm path chosen) is the suspect, not the compiled code.\n- Linux (x86_64, 4 pinned cores): same kernel +2% only, so whatever it is does not show there.\n\nNext: read the program thread's FPCR (mrs fpcr) during and after an install, and compare pow's arguments; test with the LLVM compile on a thread that restores the FP environment.","files":null}]}